# in a text join, a parenthesized piece after a rendering is read as a call of what was rendered: '$alpha (" x")' is
# '$(alpha(" x"))' - error[E13b] "F64 is not a function, and declares no Call". Correct by E13b ("(args) after any
# postfix expression calls"), but a trap where pieces are written side by side; '$alpha $(...)' or two prints work
# Status: a deliberate rule (E13b), kept as a record of the trap; olang db2af5d reports it at the parenthesis, as
# 472373d did, as error[E11b] "'(' after a piece of text calls it - to join the value inside, write '$(...)'", which
# names the way out.
fn main() ? {
    alpha F64 = 0.5
    normalized := true
    text := "alpha " $alpha (" normalized" if normalized else "")
    assert text.Len() > 0
}
